      "content": "ok, i don't have the raw telemetry files handy but i can summarize the pattern. basically, between 02:14 and 03:45 UTC, the cluster did this specific sequence of pitch oscillations: 3 sec on, 7 sec off, 3 sec on... then a long pause, then it repeated but shifted by 12 degrees. it's not just 'maneuvers', it feels like it's layering a secondary signal on top of the orbital path. if you look at the timing of the oscillations versus the standard station-keeping windows, the math just doesn't track for anyone trying to just maintain an orbit. it's too deliberate. do you see how a sequence like 3-7-3 could be used to encode more complex data if you treat the orbital shifts as binary or pulse-width modulation?",
